#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""watch-guard: a main-thread Stop hook that holds a turn open, once, when a long wait has
lapsed, a watched job ended unnoticed, or a background command was killed at its time limit.

Claude Code stops a background command at its timeout (2 hours at most), and the wake-up note
then says not to restart it, so a lead waiting on a long job can end its turn with nothing left
to wake it. This hook is the enforcement:

- Watches (`delegation-ledger wait`; delegation_common documents the file). For each of this
  session's open or acknowledged watches, including those this Claude process recorded in a
  session that isn't live (a /clear, or a watch recorded with no session), which it adopts:
  - a live waiter, or a background shell whose command holds the watch id, allows (the waiter
    records the end itself);
  - else a condition that has ended is recorded so and blocks once, even after its lapse was
    acknowledged: the dead waiter's notification never came, so the lead is told to check the
    result and report it. That is a met condition (done), a failed or stale one, a codex run
    with a stop row (done on exit 0, failed otherwise), or one that never started (failed);
  - a job that has ended in a way the guard can't judge is "ended": it isn't recorded, the
    watch stays open and blocks once (end_blocked_at), even after its lapse was acknowledged,
    saying to re-arm it, since the re-armed waiter decides. That is a codex run that ended
    without a stop row (its outcome waits on a finalize, which the waiter does), a --log
    verdict that a line further back than the guard reads could change (unseen): it reads only
    the log's last 1 MB, to keep a stop cheap, and a --log verdict, ended or unmet, that the
    log's last line, with no newline yet, would change if it is finished as it stands
    (unfinished, said as PARTIAL): the guard never matches such a line itself. A first block
    on such a watch is also the lapse's, so a later stop acknowledges it as below;
  - else it has lapsed: the condition is unmet, or the codex run still runs. A lapse
    blocks once, recording blocked_at, a digest of the stop (its prompt_id and
    last_assistant_message) and the transcript's size. A later stop, whose digest differs,
    allows, marks it acknowledged and warns in a systemMessage; later stops say nothing more.
    A twin guard in the same stop has the same digest and says nothing, even when the
    transcript grew between them. Only a payload with neither field falls back to the size
    (grown means later), then to age (over 60 s). Acknowledging it, the guard also starts a
    detached waiter on it (spawn_waiter), which watches the whole condition as any waiter does,
    for up to a day. Its exit reaches no one, so it records the end unreported, and a later
    stop says that end (below). A re-arm by the lead takes the watch over from it. One that
    stops early records why (end_error) and reopens the watch, so the next stop's lapse block
    says that error once (also on a codex run that has left the ledger), and the guard starts
    no other (respawn_refused).
  - a watch of this session recorded under a Claude process that has ended (a crash, then
    `claude --continue`) is adopted too: this process records itself as the watch's. A waiter
    whose process still runs (even with a stale heartbeat) reports to nobody, so it is marked
    waiter_unheard and blocks once with the re-arm command, unless this stop records the
    watch's end; a dead one is a lapse, as above.
  - adoption, either kind, clears the watch's lapse record and reopens an acknowledged one:
    the adopting conversation never saw the old block, so a lapse blocks once again there.
    It can't repeat, since an adopted watch is this process's in this session.
  - a watch of this session that ended (done, failed or stale) in the last 7 days while no
    Claude process was listening (its waiter was unheard or detached, or its Claude process
    has ended), and isn't reported yet, blocks once and is recorded reported. So does one
    this Claude process left in a session that isn't live (a /clear), since a detached
    waiter's end can come before the new session's first stop adopts its watch.
- Kills. Each stop reads the transcript on from where the last one stopped (kills/<sid>.json
  holds the offset; a session's first scan starts 256 KB back), searching the raw bytes for
  task notifications. One whose status is killed at the background time limit blocks once per
  task id, in either shape: a user entry between turns, or a mid-turn attachment entry. The
  first run records since as 10 minutes back, so older kills never block. A killed
  `delegation-ledger wait` folds into its watch's item when that watch has one in this stop:
  the watch its launch line names (anywhere in the output file's first 4 KB), else the one a
  --resume names, else the one watch created within 120 s after the launch (more than one:
  the one whose condition the command's --pid, --file or --log names). A killed
  `codex-delegate run|resume` folds the same way into the watch its wrapper waited as, which
  its launch line names, found the same way. With none (a sandboxed run), it doesn't fold;
  its reason says Codex keeps running, and re-arms the run's watch when one is open.

Each stop runs in three phases, so what it records it also says. Gather is read-only and
budgeted (3 s): no new item once that's spent, and a kill whose launch lookup the deadline cut
off gets the plain reason. Commit records every gathered item in one pass under
kills/<sid>.lock and watches/.lock, whose waits are bounded: a busy lock means nothing is taken
this stop. Then it prints. It decides by its own records, not stop_hook_active, so a hook
registered twice can't loop and another hook's block can't swallow this one. It FAILS OPEN:
an error on one item is logged to delegation-ledger.err and skips that item, and any other
error allows the stop.
"""
import collections
import contextlib
import datetime
import glob
import hashlib
import json
import math
import mmap
import os
import re
import sys
import time
import traceback

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, HERE)
import delegation_common as dc  # noqa: E402
import delegation_checks as checks  # noqa: E402

LEDGER = os.path.join(HERE, "delegation-ledger")
TAIL_BYTES = 256 * 1024     # a session's first transcript scan starts this far back
LOG_TAIL = 1024 * 1024      # a --log condition is checked in the log's last 1 MB
BUDGET_S = 3.0              # for gathering, under the hook's 5 s timeout
LOCK_GRACE_S = 0.5          # the commit's lock wait when gathering spent the budget
ACK_UNKNOWN_AGE_S = 60      # with no transcript size, a later stop is one this much later
FIRST_RUN_LOOKBACK_S = 600
FOLD_WINDOW_S = 120         # a fresh waiter creates its watch this soon after its launch
KILLS_PRUNE_DAYS, KILLS_TMP_DAYS = 7, 1
MARK = b"task-notification"
WATCH_REASON = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) has no live waiter and {unmet}. If a command was stopped "
                "at its time limit, don't re-run it. Re-arm the watch: delegation-ledger wait "
                "--resume {id}, with run_in_background and timeout 7200000. To stop watching "
                "it: delegation-ledger wait --drop {id}.")
UNHEARD_REASON = ("Watch {id}'s waiter was started by a Claude Code process that has ended, so "
                  "its exit won't reach you. Re-arm it: delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, "
                  "with run_in_background and timeout 7200000.")
ENDED_UNHEARD = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) ended ({state}) while no Claude Code process was "
                 "listening. Check the result and report it.")
DETACHED_ENDED = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) ended {state}: {why}. The waiter the guard left watching "
                  "it when its lapse was acknowledged recorded that, but its exit reaches no "
                  "one. Check the result and report it.{codex}")
DETACHED_CODEX = (" For codex run {run}: codex-delegate status {run} shows it, and its report is "
                  "{report}.")
DETACHED_FAILED = ("Watch {id} ({desc}): the guard's detached waiter failed: {why}. Re-arm to see "
                   "the error: delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, with run_in_background and "
                   "timeout 7200000. Or drop the watch: delegation-ledger wait --drop {id}.")
PARTIAL = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) looks {looks} ({why}). But {log}'s last line has no newline yet "
           "and matches {lines}, and it may be the start of a longer line, so the guard can't "
           "tell. Re-arm the watch and its waiter reads that line once it settles and reports "
           "the outcome: delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, with run_in_background and "
           "timeout 7200000. To stop watching it: delegation-ledger wait --drop {id}.")
DETACHED_SLACK_S = 600  # a detached waiter that ran this close to its max reached it
CODEX_UNSTARTED = ("Watch {id} ({desc}): codex run {run} never started, since its wrapper "
                   "stopped before Codex did, so the watch is recorded failed. To retry, "
                   "{restart}, with run_in_background and timeout 7200000.")
MET_REASON = ("Watch {id} ({desc}): {cond} is met, but its waiter had stopped, so no "
              "notification came. Check the result and report it.")
JOB_ENDED = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) ended {state}: {why}. Its waiter had stopped, so no "
             "notification came. Check the result and report it.")
CODEX_ENDED = ("Watch {id} ({desc}): codex run {run} has ended, but its waiter had stopped, "
               "so the run isn't finalized. Re-arm the watch and its waiter finalizes it: "
               "delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, with run_in_background and timeout "
               "7200000. To stop watching it: delegation-ledger wait --drop {id}.")
UNCERTAIN = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) looks {looks} ({why}). But {log} is longer than the last "
             "1 MB the guard reads, and {lines} could be further back, so it can't tell. Re-arm "
             "the watch and its waiter reads the whole log and reports the outcome: "
             "delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, with run_in_background and timeout "
             "7200000. To stop watching it: delegation-ledger wait --drop {id}.")
CODEX_FAILED = ("codex run {run} ended with exit {exit}, but its waiter had stopped, so no "
                "notification came. Check its report and report it.")
KILL_REASON = ('Background command "{desc}" was stopped at its time limit. If it was waiting '
               "on a job that's still running, don't re-run it: wait with delegation-ledger "
               "wait (--pid, --file or --log), with run_in_background and timeout 7200000. If "
               "it was the job itself, report that it was stopped. If you've already handled "
               "it, end your turn.")
CODEX_KILL = ('Codex wrapper "{desc}" was stopped at its time limit, but Codex keeps running. '
              "Don't re-run it. Wait on it with delegation-ledger wait --codex {run} "
              "(codex-delegate status lists the run), with run_in_background and timeout "
              "7200000.")
CODEX_UNKNOWN = ('Codex wrapper "{desc}" was stopped at its time limit, but Codex keeps '
                 "running. Don't re-run it. Find the run with codex-delegate status, then wait on "
                 "it with delegation-ledger wait --codex and its run id, with run_in_background "
                 "and timeout 7200000.")
CODEX_REARM = ('Codex wrapper "{desc}" was stopped at its time limit, but Codex keeps running. '
               "Don't re-run it. Re-arm the run's watch: `delegation-ledger wait --resume "
               "{wid}`, with run_in_background and timeout 7200000.")
ACK_MESSAGE = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) still has no waiter; it's acknowledged. Re-arm with "
               "delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, or drop it.")
DETACHED_ACK = ("Watch {id} ({desc}) still has no waiter of the lead's; it's acknowledged, and "
                "the guard started a detached waiter on it, whose end a later stop says (it "
                "logs a refusal or an error to delegation-ledger.err). To be woken when it ends "
                "instead, re-arm with delegation-ledger wait --resume {id}, or drop it.")
TASK_RE = re.compile(r"<task-id>([^<]+)</task-id>")
TOOL_USE_RE = re.compile(r"<tool-use-id>([^<]+)</tool-use-id>")
OUTPUT_RE = re.compile(r"<output-file>([^<]+)</output-file>")
LAUNCH_RE = re.compile(r"codex-delegate: run (\S+), watch (\S+)\. ")
WAIT_LAUNCH_RE = re.compile(r"delegation-ledger: watch (\S+)\. If this command is stopped")
LAUNCH_BYTES = 4096         # a launch line is looked for this far into a killed command's output
SUMMARY_RE = re.compile(r"<summary>(.*?)</summary>", re.S)
DESC_RE = re.compile(r'^Background command "(.*)" was stopped', re.S)
WAITER_RE = re.compile(r"\bdelegation-ledger\s+wait\b")
RESUME_RE = re.compile(r"\bdelegation-ledger\s+wait\b.*?--resume[=\s]+['\"]?([^\s'\"]+)")
CODEX_RE = re.compile(r"\bcodex-delegate\s+(run|resume)\b(?:\s+['\"]?([^\s'\"]+))?")
ARG_RE = {opt: re.compile(rf"--{opt}[=\s]+['\"]?([^\s'\"]+)") for opt in ("pid", "file", "log")}

Notice = collections.namedtuple("Notice", "task tool_use desc ts pos output")
# A watch's gathered item: kind "end" (the job ended unnoticed: record `state`, say `text`),
# "lapse" (block once with `text`, or acknowledge), "ended" (an end the guard can't judge: a
# codex run ended with its finalize owed, a --log verdict a line further back than it reads
# could change (UNCERTAIN), or one the log's unterminated last line could (PARTIAL); block once
# with `text`, acknowledged or not, by end_blocked_at, then as a lapse) or "report" (an ended
# watch nobody heard: record it reported, say `text`). The commit re-reads a lapse's or a codex
# end's text under the lock, so a detached waiter's error recorded since is what it says.
Pending = collections.namedtuple("Pending", "kind w text state")
# A kill's gathered item: its notice, its text (None when it isn't said), and the watch it
# folds into (or None).
Kill = collections.namedtuple("Kill", "notice text fold")


def iso(t):
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(t))


def log(what, exc=None):
    """Log the exception being handled, or `exc`, with a short traceback."""
    tb = ("".join(traceback.format_exception(exc, limit=3)) if exc is not None
          else traceback.format_exc(limit=3))
    checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: {what}: {tb}")


def file_size(path):
    try:
        return os.path.getsize(path)
    except (OSError, TypeError):
        return None


# ---- gather: watches ----

def log_tail_cursor(cond):
    """A scan_log cursor at the first whole line of the log's last LOG_TAIL bytes, with "tail"
    True when that cut anything off."""
    cursor = dict(dc.log_cursor(), tail=False)
    try:
        with open(cond["log"], "rb") as f:
            size = os.fstat(f.fileno()).st_size
            if size > LOG_TAIL:
                f.seek(size - LOG_TAIL - 1)
                f.readline()  # the rest of the line the cut fell in, or just a newline
                cursor.update(offset=f.tell(), tail=True)
    except OSError:
        pass  # no log yet: scan_log says so
    return cursor


def watch_pending(w, shells, now):
    """What one watch would add, read-only: None, or a Pending. Every way its condition can end
    that the guard can judge is an end, which blocks once and resolves the watch even after its
    lapse was acknowledged, so a job that ends badly is said too. One it can't judge is
    "ended", which blocks once too, acknowledged or not, and leaves the watch open for the
    re-armed waiter to decide: a codex run that ended with no stop row (its finalize is owed),
    a --log verdict that a line further back than the log's last LOG_TAIL bytes could change
    (unseen), and one, ended or unmet, that the log's last line, with no newline yet, could
    (unfinished). Only a condition still unmet is a lapse. A codex run gone from the ledger
    raises Undecidable, which skips this watch, unless its detached waiter recorded an error,
    which is then said as the lapse."""
    wid, cond = w["id"], w.get("condition") or {}
    if dc.waiter_alive(w, now) or any(wid in c for c in shells):
        return None
    desc = w.get("description") or ""
    met = MET_REASON.format(id=wid, desc=desc, cond=dc.condition_text(cond))
    # A detached waiter's failure says why, and re-arm or drop.
    lapsed = detached_failure(w) or WATCH_REASON.format(
        id=wid, desc=desc, unmet=f"{dc.condition_text(cond)} is unmet")
    if "codex" in cond:
        run = cond["codex"]
        try:
            stage, e = dc.codex_progress(run)
        except dc.Undecidable:
            if not detached_failure(w):
                raise
            return Pending("lapse", w, lapsed, None)  # most likely why it failed: say that
        if stage == "stopped" and e.get("exit") == 0:
            return Pending("end", w, met, "done")
        if stage == "stopped":
            return Pending("end", w, CODEX_FAILED.format(run=run, exit=e.get("exit")), "failed")
        if stage == "unstarted":
            return Pending("end", w, CODEX_UNSTARTED.format(
                id=wid, desc=desc, run=run, restart=dc.codex_restart(e)), "failed")
        if stage == "ended":  # watch_verdict's done: Codex has ended, its finalize still owed
            return Pending("ended", w, codex_ended_text(w), None)
        return Pending("lapse", w, lapsed, None)
    cursor = log_tail_cursor(cond) if cond.get("log") else None
    # Final, so a last line with no newline yet is never matched here (unfinished says it).
    verdict, why = dc.watch_verdict(w, cursor, now, settle=False)
    looks = f"{why} is met" if verdict == "done" else f"{why} is unmet" if not verdict else why
    lines = unseen(verdict, cond, cursor)
    if lines:
        return Pending("ended", w, UNCERTAIN.format(
            id=wid, desc=desc, looks=verdict, why=looks, log=cond["log"],
            lines="a line matching " + " or ".join(f"/{p}/" for p in lines)), None)
    lines = unfinished(verdict, cond, cursor)
    if lines:
        return Pending("ended", w, PARTIAL.format(
            id=wid, desc=desc, looks=verdict or "unmet", why=looks, log=cond["log"],
            lines=" or ".join(f"/{p}/" for p in lines)), None)
    if verdict == "done":
        return Pending("end", w, met, "done")
    if verdict:  # failed or stale
        return Pending("end", w, JOB_ENDED.format(id=wid, desc=desc, state=verdict, why=why),
                       verdict)
    return Pending("lapse", w, lapsed, None)


def unseen(verdict, cond, cursor):
    """The --done and --fail patterns whose line, further back in the log than the guard read
    (cursor["tail"]: the log is longer than LOG_TAIL), would change an ended `verdict`, so only
    the re-armed waiter, which reads the whole log, can decide: [] when the whole log was read,
    or nothing further back could change it. A fail line in the tail fails it whatever lies
    further back. Otherwise: for done, a fail line; for failed, the done line when it is all
    the condition lacks (none in the tail, its pids gone, its --file there); for stale, that
    done line, and a fail line, which the waiter would read first and call it failed. An unmet
    condition is a lapse."""
    if verdict is None or not (cursor and cursor["tail"]) or cursor["failed"]:
        return []
    done, fail = cond.get("done"), cond.get("fail")
    only_done = (done and not cursor["done"]
                 and (not cond.get("file") or os.path.exists(cond["file"]))
                 and not any(dc.pid_alive(p.get("pid"), p.get("start"))
                             for p in cond.get("pids") or []))
    found = {"done": [fail], "failed": [only_done and done],
             "stale": [only_done and done, fail]}[verdict]
    return [p for p in found if p]


def unfinished(verdict, cond, cursor):
    """The --done and --fail patterns that the log's last line, with no newline yet, matches
    and that would change `verdict` if that line is finished as it stands. The guard never
    matches such a line itself (scan_log's settle=False), since its verdict is final and the
    line may be a fragment the writer hasn't flushed the rest of: for done, a fail line; for
    failed, the done line when it is all the condition lacks; for stale or unmet (None),
    either. Unmet matters on a watch with no pids and no --stale, whose verdict nothing else
    ends while the line waits for a newline."""
    partial = cursor.get("partial") if cursor else ""
    if not partial or cursor["failed"]:
        return []
    done, fail = cond.get("done"), cond.get("fail")
    hit = {p: bool(p and re.search(p, partial)) for p in (done, fail)}
    only_done = (hit[done] and not cursor["done"]
                 and (not cond.get("file") or os.path.exists(cond["file"]))
                 and not any(dc.pid_alive(p.get("pid"), p.get("start"))
                             for p in cond.get("pids") or []))
    either = [only_done and done, hit[fail] and fail]
    found = {"done": [hit[fail] and fail], "failed": [only_done and done], "stale": either,
             None: either}[verdict]
    return [p for p in found if p]


def detached_failure(w):
    """DETACHED_FAILED for watch w when the guard's detached waiter recorded why it stopped
    with no end (end_error), else None."""
    if w.get("waiter_detached") and w.get("end_error"):
        return DETACHED_FAILED.format(id=w["id"], desc=w.get("description") or "",
                                      why=w["end_error"])
    return None


def codex_ended_text(w):
    """What an "ended" codex watch's block says: its detached waiter's failure while no block
    on the watch has said anything since (blocked_at and end_blocked_at unset; a re-arm
    finalizes the run either way), else CODEX_ENDED. So a failure said as a lapse isn't said
    again when Codex then ends, and the end is."""
    failure = detached_failure(w)
    if failure and w.get("blocked_at") is None and w.get("end_blocked_at") is None:
        return failure
    return CODEX_ENDED.format(id=w["id"], desc=w.get("description") or "",
                              run=(w.get("condition") or {}).get("codex"))


def respawn_refused(w):
    """Why the guard mustn't start a detached waiter on acknowledged watch w, or None. One is
    started for a watch that never had one, and again only after the last one ran to its max
    (DETACHED_MAX_MIN, by its last heartbeat since detached_at): a detached waiter that died
    early (an error, nothing it could decide, a SIGKILL) would die again, and each death is
    another lapse block. One recorded before detached_at existed counts as having run to its
    max."""
    if not w.get("waiter_detached") or w.get("detached_at") is None:
        return None
    try:
        ran = (dc.parse_iso(w["waiter_heartbeat"]).timestamp()
               - dc.parse_iso(w["detached_at"]).timestamp())
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        ran = 0
    if ran >= dc.DETACHED_MAX_MIN * 60 - DETACHED_SLACK_S:
        return None
    why = f": {w['end_error']}" if w.get("end_error") else ""
    return (f"its detached waiter stopped after {ran / 60:.0f} min, before its "
            f"{dc.DETACHED_MAX_MIN} min max{why}; not starting another")


def spawn_waiter(w, sid, me):
    """Start a detached waiter on watch w, which this stop acknowledged: `delegation-ledger
    wait --resume <id> --detached <sid>`, with --claude <pid>:<procStart> when this hook knows
    its Claude process, a fixed argv and no shell. It is double-forked (fork, setsid, fork, and
    the middle process exits at once), so it is reparented away from this hook at once, and a
    kill of the hook at its timeout can't reach it. Its stdin, stdout and stderr are /dev/null
    and it holds no other descriptor of the hook's, so Claude Code sees the hook's output end
    with the hook. True once it has exec'd; False, logged, when it couldn't start, and the stop
    goes on. The waiter itself checks the watch is still acknowledged in this session with no
    live waiter, and logs a refusal."""
    argv = [sys.executable, LEDGER, "wait", "--resume", w["id"], "--detached", sid]
    if dc.is_positive(w.get("poll_s")):
        argv += ["--poll", str(w["poll_s"])]
    if me is not None:
        argv += ["--claude", f"{me[0]}:{me[1]}"]
    try:
        r, report = os.pipe()  # close-on-exec: EOF once the waiter has exec'd, else why not
        sys.stdout.flush()
        sys.stderr.flush()
        middle = os.fork()
        if middle == 0:
            try:
                os.close(r)
                os.setsid()
                if os.fork() == 0:
                    exec_detached(argv, report)
            except BaseException as ex:  # noqa: BLE001  said down the pipe: no waiter started
                with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                    os.write(report, f"{type(ex).__name__}: {ex}".encode())
            finally:
                os._exit(0)
        os.close(report)
        os.waitpid(middle, 0)
        with os.fdopen(r, "rb") as f:
            err = f.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  logged; the stop goes on
        log(f"watch {w['id']}: starting its detached waiter")
        return False
    if err:
        checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: watch {w['id']}: its detached waiter didn't start: {err}")
        return False
    return True


def exec_detached(argv, report):
    """In spawn_waiter's grandchild: /dev/null for stdin, stdout and stderr, every other
    inherited descriptor closed but `report`, the root as its directory, then exec argv. When
    that fails, it writes why to `report` and exits. Never returns."""
    try:
        null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_RDWR)
        for fd in (0, 1, 2):
            os.dup2(null, fd)
        os.closerange(3, report)
        os.closerange(report + 1, os.sysconf("SC_OPEN_MAX"))
        os.chdir("/")
        os.execv(argv[0], argv)
    except BaseException as ex:  # noqa: BLE001  said down the pipe, as nothing else can be
        with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
            os.write(report, f"{type(ex).__name__}: {ex}".encode())
    finally:
        os._exit(127)


# ---- gather: kills ----

def entry_time(e):
    """An entry's timestamp in epoch seconds, or None (it carries milliseconds)."""
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(e["timestamp"].replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except (KeyError, AttributeError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def text_of(content):
    if isinstance(content, str):
        return content
    if isinstance(content, list):
        return "".join(c["text"] for c in content
                       if isinstance(c, dict) and isinstance(c.get("text"), str))
    return ""


def is_task(origin):
    return isinstance(origin, dict) and origin.get("kind") == "task-notification"


def parse_notice(line, pos):
    """A Notice when this transcript line is a kill at the background time limit, else None.
    Two shapes count: a user entry with a task-notification origin (between turns) and an
    attachment entry whose attachment has one (mid-turn). Its rendered copies, and the
    queue-operation entry, don't."""
    try:
        e = json.loads(line)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if not isinstance(e, dict):
        return None
    attachment = e.get("attachment") if isinstance(e.get("attachment"), dict) else {}
    if e.get("type") == "user" and is_task(e.get("origin")):
        msg = e.get("message")
        text = text_of(msg.get("content") if isinstance(msg, dict) else None)
    elif e.get("type") == "attachment" and is_task(attachment.get("origin")):
        text = attachment.get("prompt") if isinstance(attachment.get("prompt"), str) else ""
    else:
        return None
    task, ts = TASK_RE.search(text), entry_time(e) or entry_time(attachment)
    if ("<status>killed</status>" not in text or "background time limit" not in text
            or task is None or ts is None):
        return None
    tool, summary = TOOL_USE_RE.search(text), SUMMARY_RE.search(text)
    output = OUTPUT_RE.search(text)
    named = DESC_RE.match(summary.group(1)) if summary else None
    desc = named.group(1) if named else (summary.group(1) if summary else task.group(1))
    return Notice(task.group(1), tool.group(1) if tool else None, desc, ts, pos,
                  output.group(1) if output else None)


def scan(path, start, cut):
    """(notices, end, length) for the complete lines of the transcript from byte `start` on:
    its kill notices, one per task id with its line's offset, the offset just past the last
    complete line, and the length it read (a line may land after decide read the size). With
    `cut`, start may fall inside a line, which is then skipped. It searches the raw bytes and
    parses only the lines holding a task notification, so a long region is cheap."""
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        size = os.fstat(f.fileno()).st_size
        if size <= start:
            return [], start, size
        with mmap.mmap(f.fileno(), 0, access=mmap.ACCESS_READ) as m:
            length = len(m)
            begin = start
            if cut and start > 0 and m[start - 1:start] != b"\n":
                nl = m.find(b"\n", start)
                begin = len(m) if nl < 0 else nl + 1
            last = m.rfind(b"\n", begin)
            end = begin if last < 0 else last + 1
            notices, hit = {}, m.find(MARK, begin, end)
            while hit >= 0:
                head = m.rfind(b"\n", begin, hit)
                head = begin if head < 0 else head + 1
                tail = m.find(b"\n", hit, end)
                n = parse_notice(m[head:tail].decode("utf-8", "replace"), head)
                if n is not None and n.task not in notices:
                    notices[n.task] = n
                hit = m.find(MARK, tail + 1, end)
    return sorted(notices.values(), key=lambda n: n.pos), end, length


def kills_dir():
    return os.path.join(dc.state_dir(), "kills")


def kills_path(sid, ext=".json"):
    safe = re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]", "_", sid).lstrip(".") or "_"
    return os.path.join(kills_dir(), safe + ext)


def read_kill_record(sid):
    try:
        with open(kills_path(sid)) as f:
            rec = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return rec if isinstance(rec, dict) else {}


def record_offset(rec, transcript):
    o = rec.get("offset") if rec.get("transcript") == transcript else None
    return o if isinstance(o, int) and not isinstance(o, bool) and o >= 0 else None


def prune_kills(now, sid):
    """Remove records (and their locks) unwritten for KILLS_PRUNE_DAYS, orphan locks as old,
    and temp files over a day old. It runs unlocked, so it never touches this session's own
    record or lock, which a twin guard may hold."""
    old, own = now - KILLS_PRUNE_DAYS * 86400, {kills_path(sid), kills_path(sid, ".lock")}
    for pattern, cutoff in (("*.json", old), ("*.lock", old),
                            (".*.tmp", now - KILLS_TMP_DAYS * 86400)):
        for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(kills_dir(), pattern)):
            try:
                if p in own or os.path.getmtime(p) >= cutoff:
                    continue
                if p.endswith(".lock") and os.path.exists(p[:-5] + ".json"):
                    continue  # its session still records kills
                os.remove(p)
            except OSError:
                pass


def launched(path, tool_use, spent):
    """(command, epoch) of the Bash tool_use with this id, searched through the whole
    transcript (the launch can be hours back); None when it isn't there or the budget ran out
    on the way."""
    if not tool_use:
        return None
    with open(path, errors="replace") as f:
        for i, line in enumerate(f):
            if i % 512 == 0 and spent():
                return None
            if tool_use not in line or '"tool_use"' not in line:
                continue
            try:
                e = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            msg = e.get("message") if isinstance(e, dict) else None
            for c in (msg.get("content") if isinstance(msg, dict) else None) or []:
                if isinstance(c, dict) and c.get("type") == "tool_use" and c.get("id") == tool_use:
                    cmd = (c.get("input") or {}).get("command")
                    return (cmd, entry_time(e)) if isinstance(cmd, str) else None
    return None


def names_condition(command, w, cwd):
    """Whether the waiter command's --pid, --file and --log (at least one) all match watch
    w's condition; relative paths resolve against the session's cwd."""
    cond = w.get("condition") or {}
    args = {opt: rx.findall(command) for opt, rx in ARG_RE.items()}
    if not any(args.values()):
        return False

    def path(p):
        return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(cwd or "", os.path.expanduser(p)))
    pids = {p.get("pid") for p in cond.get("pids") or []}
    return (all(p.isdigit() and int(p) in pids for p in args["pid"])
            and all(path(p) == cond.get("file") for p in args["file"])
            and all(path(p) == cond.get("log") for p in args["log"]))


def fold_target(command, at, watches, cwd):
    """The watch a killed `delegation-ledger wait` waited on: the one its --resume names, else
    the session's one unresolved watch created within FOLD_WINDOW_S after its launch, and when
    several were, the one whose condition the command names."""
    named = RESUME_RE.search(command)
    if named:
        return named.group(1)
    fits = created_after(watches, at)
    if len(fits) > 1:
        fits = [w for w in fits if names_condition(command, w, cwd)]
    return fits[0]["id"] if len(fits) == 1 else None


def created_after(watches, at):
    """The watches created within FOLD_WINDOW_S after a launch at `at` (None: no launch)."""
    fits = []
    for w in watches if at is not None else ():
        try:
            created = dc.parse_iso(w["created"]).timestamp()
        except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
            continue
        if math.floor(at) <= created <= at + FOLD_WINDOW_S:
            fits.append(w)
    return fits


def output_head(output, spent):
    """The first LAUNCH_BYTES of a killed command's output file, where a waiter or a
    codex-delegate prints its launch line before anything slow; "" with none, or once the
    budget is spent."""
    if not output or spent():
        return ""
    try:
        with open(output, "rb") as f:
            return f.read(LAUNCH_BYTES).decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except OSError:
        return ""


def launch_of(output, spent):
    """(run, watch) from a codex-delegate's launch line; (None, None) with none (a sandboxed
    run, or one refused before it launched)."""
    m = LAUNCH_RE.search(output_head(output, spent))
    return (m.group(1), m.group(2)) if m else (None, None)


def codex_target(match, output, spent):
    """(fold, run) for a killed `codex-delegate run|resume` (its CODEX_RE match): the watch and
    run its launch line names. With no launch line it doesn't fold, and a resume's run is the
    one its run or thread id names."""
    run, wid = launch_of(output, spent)
    if wid is not None:
        return wid, run
    if match.group(1) != "resume":
        return None, None
    key = match.group(2) if match.group(2) and not match.group(2).startswith("-") else None
    e = dc.find_codex(key) if key else None
    return None, (e["run_id"] if e else key)


def map_kill(n, transcript, watches, cwd, spent):
    """A Kill for one new notice: the plain reason when its launch can't be found in time."""
    plain = Kill(n, KILL_REASON.format(desc=n.desc), None)
    try:
        found = launched(transcript, n.tool_use, spent)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  can't map it, so say it as a plain kill
        log(f"kill {n.task}")
        return plain
    command, at = found or ("", None)
    if WAITER_RE.search(command):
        named = WAIT_LAUNCH_RE.search(output_head(n.output, spent))
        fold = named.group(1) if named else fold_target(command, at, watches, cwd)
        return plain._replace(fold=fold, text=None if resolved(n, fold) else plain.text)
    codex = CODEX_RE.search(command)
    if codex:
        fold, run = codex_target(codex, n.output, spent)
        if resolved(n, fold):
            return Kill(n, None, fold)
        open_watch = dc.codex_watch(run) if fold is None and run else None
        wid = fold or (open_watch or {}).get("id")
        text = (CODEX_REARM.format(desc=n.desc, wid=wid) if wid else
                CODEX_KILL.format(desc=n.desc, run=run) if run else
                CODEX_UNKNOWN.format(desc=n.desc))
        return Kill(n, text, fold)
    return plain


def resolved(n, wid):
    """Whether the watch a kill folds into is resolved (dropped, or ended): the lead already
    handled it, by a codex-delegate cancel or a drop, so saying the kill would only ask for a
    re-arm that is refused. Logged when so."""
    try:
        w = dc.read_watch(wid) if wid else None
    except ValueError:
        return False
    if w and w.get("state") not in dc.UNRESOLVED:
        checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: kill of task {n.task} folds into watch {wid}, which is "
                         f"{w.get('state')}, so it isn't said")
        return True
    return False


def kill_pending(sid, transcript, now, spent, watches, cwd):
    """(kills, scan) read-only: the new kills from the transcript, read on from the recorded
    offset, and {"since", "end", "length"} for the commit, length being what the scan read.
    It takes notices until the budget is spent; end then stops at the first one left, so the
    next stop reads it again."""
    rec = read_kill_record(sid)
    try:
        since = dc.parse_iso(rec["since"]).timestamp()
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        since = now - FIRST_RUN_LOOKBACK_S
    seen = {s for s in rec.get("seen") or [] if isinstance(s, str)}
    stored, size = record_offset(rec, transcript), file_size(transcript)
    notices, end, length = [], stored, None
    if size is not None:
        if stored is not None and stored > size:
            stored = 0  # rewritten or replaced: read it again from the top
        start, cut = ((stored, False) if stored is not None
                      else (max(0, size - TAIL_BYTES), True))
        notices, end, length = scan(transcript, start, cut)
    kills = []
    for n in notices:
        if n.task in seen or n.ts < since:
            continue
        if spent():
            end = n.pos
            break
        kills.append(map_kill(n, transcript, watches, cwd, spent))
    return kills, {"since": since, "end": end, "length": length}


# ---- commit ----

def stop_digest(data):
    """The stop's identity: sha256 (first 16 hex) of its prompt_id and last_assistant_message,
    the same for twin guards in one stop. None when the payload has neither."""
    parts = [data.get(k) for k in ("prompt_id", "last_assistant_message")]
    if all(p is None for p in parts):
        return None
    raw = json.dumps(parts, ensure_ascii=False, default=str).encode("utf-8")
    return hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()[:16]


def crashed(w, sid, me):
    """Whether watch w is this session's and the Claude process it was recorded under has
    ended (a crash, then `claude --continue` in this process, `me`)."""
    return me is not None and w.get("session_id") == sid and dc.claude_gone(w)


def adoption(sid, me, now):
    """The adoption step of the commit pass, as (adopt, settle). adopt(wid) is the update that
    adopts watch wid, checked again under the lock:
    - one this process left in a session that isn't live (dc.left_by_clear: a /clear, or a
      watch recorded with no session) moves into this session. One another session re-armed,
      or whose old session came back, since it was gathered stays where it is;
    - a crashed one records this process as its Claude process, and a waiter whose process
      still runs, even with a stale heartbeat, is marked waiter_unheard: its exit goes to the
      process that ended.
    Either way the watch's lapse record is cleared and an acknowledged lapse reopened
    (dc.unblock), since the adopting conversation never saw them: the guard blocks once on it
    afresh. That can't repeat, since an adopted watch is this process's, in this session.
    settle(lost) logs each adoption whose watch was written (not in `lost`) and returns the ids
    of those whose waiter still runs but reports to nobody."""
    moved, revived, unheard = [], [], []

    def adopt(wid):
        def move(cur):
            if cur.get("state") not in dc.UNRESOLVED:
                return
            if cur.get("session_id") != sid:
                if dc.left_by_clear(cur, me, dc.live_sessions()):
                    moved.append((wid, cur.get("session_id")))
                    cur["session_id"] = sid
                    dc.unblock(cur)
            elif crashed(cur, sid, me):
                revived.append((wid, cur.get("claude_pid")))
                cur.update(claude_pid=me[0], claude_start=me[1])
                dc.unblock(cur)
                # By its process, not its heartbeat: a waiter suspended past its heartbeat (a
                # paused VM) still exits to the ended process when it resumes.
                if dc.pid_alive(cur.get("waiter_pid"), cur.get("waiter_start")):
                    cur["waiter_unheard"] = True
                    unheard.append(wid)
        return move

    def settle(lost):
        for wid, old in moved:
            if wid not in lost:
                checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: adopted watch {wid} from session {old}: the "
                                 "same Claude process (a /clear, or a watch recorded with no "
                                 "session)")
        for wid, old in revived:
            if wid not in lost:
                checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: adopted watch {wid} from Claude process {old}, "
                                 "which has ended (a crash, then claude --continue)")
        return [wid for wid in unheard if wid not in lost]
    return adopt, settle


def then(first, second):
    """One watch update that runs `first`, then `second`, on the same read of the watch."""
    def fn(cur):
        first(cur)
        second(cur)
    return fn


def ended_text(w):
    """What the guard says of watch w, which ended while nobody listened: a detached waiter's
    end with how it ended (end_why), and for a codex run, where to look."""
    desc = w.get("description") or ""
    if not w.get("waiter_detached"):
        return ENDED_UNHEARD.format(id=w["id"], desc=desc, state=w["state"])
    run, codex = (w.get("condition") or {}).get("codex"), ""
    if run is not None:
        try:
            e = dc.find_codex(run) or {}
            report = (os.path.join(e["out"], "report.json") if e.get("out")
                      else "named by codex-delegate status")
            codex = DETACHED_CODEX.format(run=run, report=report)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  the end is still said, without where to look
            log(f"watch {w['id']}: codex run {run}'s report")
    return DETACHED_ENDED.format(id=w["id"], desc=desc, state=w["state"],
                                 why=w.get("end_why") or "no reason recorded", codex=codex)


def ended_unheard(w, sid, me, live, now):
    """Whether watch w ended while nobody listened (dc.ended_unheard) and is this guard's to
    say: this session's, or one this Claude process left in a session that isn't live
    (dc.left_by_clear), as a detached waiter's end is when it comes after a /clear but before
    the new session's first stop adopts the watch."""
    return ((w.get("session_id") == sid or dc.left_by_clear(w, me, live))
            and dc.ended_unheard(w, now))


def later_stop(cur, stop, size, now):
    """Whether this stop comes after the one that blocked on the watch: its digest differs.
    Only when either stop's payload had neither field, the transcript grew, or, with no size
    to compare, the block is over ACK_UNKNOWN_AGE_S old."""
    blocked_stop = cur.get("blocked_stop")
    if stop is not None and blocked_stop is not None:
        return stop != blocked_stop
    blocked = cur.get("blocked_size")
    if isinstance(size, int) and isinstance(blocked, int):
        return size > blocked
    try:
        return now - dc.parse_iso(cur["blocked_at"]).timestamp() > ACK_UNKNOWN_AGE_S
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return True


def commit(sid, transcript, adopted, me, pendings, kills, scan_out, stop, size, now, until,
           clock):
    """Record everything gathered in one pass, under kills/<sid>.lock and then watches/.lock
    (each wait bounded by `until`), and return (items, acknowledged watches). The adoptions
    and every watch's
    item go in one update_watches, so LockBusy, which only a lock wait raises, means nothing is
    recorded or said this stop. Each watch is re-read under the lock, so a waiter that re-armed
    meanwhile, a twin guard that recorded first, or a watch that wasn't adopted after all (it's
    in another session), wins. A watch whose update fails is logged and unsaid, adoption
    included, and comes up again next stop; the rest, and the kills, go on."""
    items, acked, itemized, outcomes, ended, texts = [], [], set(), {}, set(), {}

    def record(p):
        wid = p.w["id"]

        def fn(cur):
            if p.kind == "report":  # gathered as this guard's to say; once, still unreported
                if dc.ended_unheard(cur, now):
                    cur["reported"] = True
                    outcomes[wid] = "block"
                return
            if (cur.get("state") not in dc.UNRESOLVED or dc.waiter_alive(cur, now)
                    or cur.get("session_id") != sid):
                return  # dropped, ended, re-armed or not adopted since it was gathered
            if p.kind == "end":
                cur.update(state=p.state, ended=iso(now), reported=True)
                outcomes[wid] = "block"
                ended.add(wid)
            elif p.kind == "ended" and cur.get("end_blocked_at") is None:
                # The text by the watch as it is now: a detached waiter's error recorded since
                # it was gathered is said, once (codex_ended_text).
                if "codex" in (cur.get("condition") or {}):
                    texts[wid] = codex_ended_text(cur)
                # This stop is the block now, so a twin guard in it doesn't acknowledge, and a
                # first block on the watch is also its lapse's.
                cur.update(end_blocked_at=iso(now), blocked_at=iso(now), blocked_stop=stop,
                           blocked_size=size)
                outcomes[wid] = "block"
            elif cur.get("blocked_at") is None:
                if p.kind == "lapse":  # as above: this block is the error's only one
                    texts[wid] = detached_failure(cur) or p.text
                cur.update(blocked_at=iso(now), blocked_stop=stop, blocked_size=size)
                outcomes[wid] = "block"
            elif cur.get("state") == "open" and later_stop(cur, stop, size, now):
                cur["state"] = "acknowledged"
                outcomes[wid] = "ack"
        return fn

    adopt, settle = adoption(sid, me, now)
    fns = {wid: adopt(wid) for wid in adopted}
    for p in pendings:  # an adopted watch's item runs on what its adoption left
        wid = p.w["id"]
        fns[wid] = then(fns[wid], record(p)) if wid in fns else record(p)
    with dc.file_lock(kills_path(sid, ".lock"), until, clock):
        _, failed = dc.update_watches(fns, until, clock) if fns else ({}, [])
        for wid in settle({wid for wid, _ in failed}):
            if wid not in ended:  # "re-arm it" would contradict the end said below
                items.append(UNHEARD_REASON.format(id=wid))
                itemized.add(wid)
        for wid, exc in failed:
            outcomes.pop(wid, None)
            log(f"watch {wid}", exc)
        for p in pendings:
            outcome = outcomes.get(p.w["id"])
            if outcome == "block":
                items.append(texts.get(p.w["id"], p.text))
                itemized.add(p.w["id"])
            elif outcome == "ack":
                acked.append(p.w)
        if scan_out is not None:
            try:
                items += commit_kills(sid, transcript, kills, scan_out, itemized)
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  unrecorded, so unsaid; the watches stand
                log("kills")
    return items, acked


def commit_kills(sid, transcript, kills, scan_out, itemized):
    """Record the gathered kills as seen and move the offset on, and return their items. The
    record is re-read first: a twin guard may have taken some already. An offset past what the
    scan read (a rewritten transcript) is dropped."""
    rec = read_kill_record(sid)
    seen = {s for s in rec.get("seen") or [] if isinstance(s, str)}
    taken = [k for k in kills if k.notice.task not in seen]
    length = scan_out["length"]
    offsets = [o for o in (record_offset(rec, transcript), scan_out["end"])
               if o is not None and (length is None or o <= length)]
    try:
        since = iso(dc.parse_iso(rec["since"]).timestamp())
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        since = iso(scan_out["since"])
    new = {"session_id": sid, "since": since, "transcript": transcript,
           "seen": sorted(seen | {k.notice.task for k in taken}),
           "offset": max(offsets) if offsets else None}
    if new != rec:
        dc.write_json(kills_path(sid), new)
    return [k.text for k in taken if k.text is not None and k.fold not in itemized]


# ---- the hook ----

def render(items):
    return "[watch-guard]\n" + "\n".join(f"{n}. {text}" for n, text in enumerate(items, 1))


def decide(data, now, clock=time.monotonic):
    """None to let the stop through, else the hook output: a block whose reason numbers every
    new item, a systemMessage for the lapses it lets through (each with a detached waiter
    started on it), or both. It gathers within
    BUDGET_S (by `clock`), then commits all it gathered, so what it records it also returns.
    In a sandbox's PID namespace (pids_visible) every waiter and Claude process reads as dead,
    so it says nothing there."""
    if not dc.pids_visible():
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, dict) or data.get("agent_id"):
        return None
    sid = data.get("session_id")
    if not isinstance(sid, str) or not sid:
        return None
    deadline = clock() + BUDGET_S

    def spent():
        return clock() >= deadline
    shells = [t["command"] for t in data.get("background_tasks") or []
              if isinstance(t, dict) and isinstance(t.get("command"), str)]
    cwd = data.get("cwd") if isinstance(data.get("cwd"), str) else None
    transcript = data.get("transcript_path")
    transcript = transcript if isinstance(transcript, str) else None
    size, stop = file_size(transcript), stop_digest(data)
    try:
        prune_kills(now, sid)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        log("prune")
    me = dc.claude_process()  # this hook's Claude process; with none, nothing is adopted
    live = dc.live_sessions() if me is not None else {}
    every = [w for w in dc.all_watches() if isinstance(w.get("id"), str)]
    watches = [w for w in every if w.get("state") in dc.UNRESOLVED
               and (w.get("session_id") == sid or dc.left_by_clear(w, me, live))]
    adopted = [w["id"] for w in watches if w.get("session_id") != sid or crashed(w, sid, me)]
    pendings = []
    for i, w in enumerate(watches):
        if spent():
            checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: the {BUDGET_S:g} s budget is spent; "
                             f"{len(watches) - i} watch(es) wait for the next stop")
            break
        try:
            p = watch_pending(w, shells, now)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  dc.Undecidable or worse: skip this watch only
            log(f"watch {w['id']}")
            continue
        if p is not None:
            pendings.append(p)
    pendings += [Pending("report", w, ended_text(w), None)
                 for w in every if not spent() and ended_unheard(w, sid, me, live, now)]
    kills, scan_out = [], None
    if transcript and not spent():
        try:
            kills, scan_out = kill_pending(sid, transcript, now, spent, watches, cwd)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  the kill catch is skipped; the watches stand
            log("kills")
    if not pendings and scan_out is None and not adopted:
        return None
    try:
        items, acked = commit(sid, transcript, adopted, me, pendings, kills, scan_out, stop,
                              size, now, max(deadline, clock() + LOCK_GRACE_S), clock)
    except dc.LockBusy as ex:
        checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: {ex} stayed locked; nothing was taken this stop")
        return None
    # After the commit, so the detached waiter finds the watch acknowledged and the lock free.
    acks = []
    for w in acked:
        refused = respawn_refused(w)
        if refused:
            checks.log_error(f"watch-guard: watch {w['id']}: {refused}")
        started = not refused and spawn_waiter(w, sid, me)
        acks.append((DETACHED_ACK if started else ACK_MESSAGE).format(
            id=w["id"], desc=w.get("description") or ""))
    out = {"decision": "block", "reason": render(items)} if items else {}
    if acks:
        out["systemMessage"] = " ".join(acks)
    return out or None


def main():
    try:
        out = decide(json.loads(sys.stdin.read() or "{}"), time.time())
        if out:
            print(json.dumps(out), flush=True)  # at once: everything in it is recorded
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001  fail open: never wedge a session
        checks.log_error()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
